= Klein Paradox for Spin-0 Particles by Douglas M. Gingrich
{parent=Physics bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Douglas M. Gingrich}
{title2=University of Alberta lecture notes, page dated 18 March 2004}

The institutional lecture derives the outgoing branch, boundary amplitudes and signed charge-current balance for a scalar particle at an electrostatic step. Equations 4.142–4.155 distinguish ordinary transmission, evanescence and the negative-kinetic-energy regime; the following discussion interprets the latter through pair creation.

https://sites.ualberta.ca/~gingrich/courses/phys512/node35.html

= Quantum Correlations of Ideal Bose and Fermi Gases in the Canonical Ensemble by Kazumasa Tsutsui and Takafumi Kita
{parent=Physics bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Kazumasa Tsutsui}
{tag=Work by Takafumi Kita}
{title2=2016}
{title2=Journal of the Physical Society of Japan 85, 114603}

This primary paper distinguishes canonical ideal-gas reduced density matrices from the grand-canonical Wick factorization. It studies how particle statistics and ensemble choice affect one- and two-body correlations. Its date is later than the examination; it corroborates the state-assumption distinction rather than documenting what was available to candidates in 2013.

https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.03617

https://doi.org/10.7566/JPSJ.85.114603

= Quantal phase factors accompanying adiabatic changes by Michael Victor Berry
{parent=Physics bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Michael Victor Berry}
{title2=1984}
{title2=Proceedings of the Royal Society A 392, 45–57}

The primary paper derives the <geometric phase> accompanying adiabatic transport around a closed parameter loop. Section 4 treats a spinning particle in a slowly changing magnetic field, with <quantum phase> proportional to its magnetic quantum number and the enclosed <solid angle>.

https://michaelberryphysics.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/07/berry120.pdf

https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1984.0023

